d  States has been experiencing over the  last decade so the city has done a  number of things over the years to try  to address its traffic problem and most  recently the city is launching the  better Market Street plan  well another plan is in the works to  transform San Francisco's Market Street  it sure looks great on paper a mega  makeover of San Francisco's mid Market  Street well you will not be seeing cars  all the way back to the dawn of  automobiles Market Street has struggled  with a diverse amount of transportation  needs from pedestrians to cyclists cars  and public transportation and even back  in 1906 it could get a little hairy one  of the most iconic things about Market  Street is that it's really just kind of  a almost a free-for-all it's about 9:00  a.m. just at the end of rush hour and  we're gonna ride all the way down to the  Ferry Building an experienced Market  Street firsthand and all of its problems  you've got goobers you've got private  cars you've got delivery trucks you've  got buses  that's just blocking the way what if you  do it you've also got like a you know  historic streetcar that's just kind of  throttling along and even though Market  Street has dedicated bike lanes when  cars need to turn right off the road  those bike lanes kind of disappear and  cyclists end up kind of weaving in and  out of traffic  at best this can lead to increase  congestion and at worst it can lead to  an accident  the city's better Marcus Street plan  aims to fix us by removing private cars  from entering the road  allowing dedicated lanes for trams buses  and cyclists to run unimpeded for the  two-mile stretch as well as a large  renovation project to the pedestrian  walkway and while construction will take  years to complete the first step  removing cars is already infected ribbon  took well over a decade of debate  environmental review and design planning  they've been talking about limiting cars  on Market Street almost since cars were  invented or